## What You Can Adopt from JKK's Latest Code

### 1. 🔴 FPS Limiter (HIGH VALUE — CPU savings)

JKK added a 45 FPS frame-rate limiter in his `run()` function. This is a significant CPU optimization that prevents the visualizer from rendering every `defer` cycle (~60+ FPS unnecessarily).

```lua
-- JKK's new code in run():
local target_fps = 45
local frame_interval = 1.0 / target_fps
local last_frame_time = reaper.time_precise()

function run()
    local current_time = reaper.time_precise()
    if (current_time - last_frame_time) < frame_interval then
        reaper.defer(run)
        return
    end
    last_frame_time = current_time
    -- ... rest of rendering
end
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This is the **#1 thing to adopt**. Your MOD currently renders every defer cycle with no throttling, which burns CPU unnecessarily. Easy to add — just wrap your `run()` with the frame limiter.

### 2. 🟡 Standby Mode (MEDIUM VALUE — CPU savings when idle)

JKK has a `g_is_standby` boolean that triggers after 2 seconds of silence + no mouse interaction. When in standby, all freeze-able modules (Scope, Spectrum, Spectrogram) skip their data updates entirely.

```lua
-- JKK's standby detection:
local last_signal_time = reaper.time_precise()
local g_is_standby = false

-- In run():
if mom_val > -100 or is_mouse_in then
    last_signal_time = current_time
end
g_is_standby = (current_time - last_signal_time) > 2.0
```

Each module then checks:
```lua
local is_frozen = is_user_frozen or g_is_standby
```

> [!NOTE]
> Your MOD removed this feature. Your modules currently keep rendering even when there's no audio and no mouse interaction. Re-adding this would save CPU when the DAW is stopped.

### 3. 🟡 JSFX: Sample-rate change detection in `@sample`

JKK added a redundant sample-rate detection block inside `@sample` (in addition to `@block`):

```
srate != last_sr ? (
    hop_size = (update_interval_ms * srate / 1000)|0;
    hop_size = min(hop_size, fft_size);
    last_sr = srate;
);
```

> [!TIP]
> You already handle this in `@block`, but having it in `@sample` provides tighter response for live sample-rate changes during playback. Low risk to adopt.

### 4. 🟢 JSFX: Simpler hop_size via `update_interval_ms`

JKK's latest uses a cleaner pattern:
```
update_interval_ms = 20;
hop_size = (update_interval_ms * srate / 1000)|0;
```

Your version uses `21.33ms` which is functionally equivalent. No action needed, but you could switch to the named constant for readability.

